== 1
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

Extend the given functions continuously to zero by $w_1(0)=w_2(0)=0$. This is forced by their stated differentiability. Their derivatives at zero are both zero, since the corresponding difference quotients tend to zero.

For $x<0$, $w_2=w_2'=0$, and for $x>0$, $w_1=w_1'=0$. At zero all four quantities vanish. Thus the <Wronskian> is
$$
\boxed{W(w_1,w_2)(x)=w_1w_2'-w_1'w_2=0\quad\text{for all }x.}
$$
Nevertheless, if the constant <linear combination> $Aw_1+Bw_2$ vanishes identically, evaluation at $x=-1$ gives $-A=0$, and evaluation at $x=1$ gives $B=0$. Therefore **the two functions are <linearly independent>**.

This illustrates why a <vanishing Wronskian does not imply linear dependence> for arbitrary <differentiable functions>. Here both functions are continuously differentiable, but neither has a second derivative at zero. The common regular differential equation hypothesis in the next part is absent.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

Differentiate the <Wronskian> using the <product rule> and substitute the common <homogeneous linear differential equation>:
$$
\begin{aligned}
W'&=(y_1y_2'-y_1'y_2)'=y_1y_2''-y_1''y_2\\
&=y_1(-py_2'-qy_2)-(-py_1'-qy_1)y_2=-pW.
\end{aligned}
$$
Multiplying this first-order equation by the <integrating factor> $\exp(\int_{x_0}^x p(t)\,dt)$ and integrating gives <Abel's identity>:
$$
\boxed{W(x)=W(x_0)\exp\!\left(-\int_{x_0}^x p(t)\,dt\right).}
$$
The exponential factor is finite and nonzero for every $x$ in the given <interval>, because $p$ is continuous on the compact interval between $x_0$ and $x$. Hence $W(x_0)\ne0$ implies $W(x)\ne0$ everywhere in that interval. Using an integrating factor avoids dividing by $W$, which would assume the conclusion.

=== b
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

Interpret the <Dirac delta> coefficient as a <point interaction in a second-order differential equation>. The solution is continuous at $x=1$ and is twice differentiable on either side, where $y''=f$. Its <transmission conditions for a delta coefficient> are
$$
[y]_1=0,\qquad [y']_1=-\alpha y(1).
$$
Indeed, the <distributional second derivative across a jump> contributes $[y']_1\delta(x-1)$, which must cancel the delta coefficient because $f$ has no point source.

Put $s=y'(0)$ and $F(x)=\int_0^x(x-t)f(t)\,dt$. Integrating the ordinary equation and inserting the required derivative jump gives
$$
y(x)=c_1+sx+F(x)-\alpha y(1)(x-1)_+,\qquad y(1)=c_1+s+F(1),
$$
where $(x-1)_+=\max(x-1,0)$. This formula is continuous, has the stated jump, satisfies the equation, and parametrizes every solution with the left endpoint value. Imposing the right endpoint value gives the scalar equation
$$
\bigl[\pi-\alpha(\pi-1)\bigr]s
=c_2-c_1-F(\pi)+\alpha(\pi-1)\bigl(c_1+F(1)\bigr).
$$
Consequently a unique $s$, and hence a unique solution for every $f,c_1,c_2$, exists precisely when
$$
\boxed{\alpha\ne\alpha_*:=\frac\pi{\pi-1}.}
$$
At the exceptional value, the nonzero homogeneous solution with zero endpoint values is
$$
\phi(x)=\begin{cases}x,&0\leq x\leq1,\\(\pi-x)/(\pi-1),&1\leq x\leq\pi.\end{cases}
$$
It has derivative jump $-\pi/(\pi-1)=-\alpha_*\phi(1)$. Thus uniqueness fails. More precisely, there are infinitely many solutions if
$$
\int_0^\pi\phi(x)f(x)\,dx=c_1+\frac{c_2}{\pi-1},
$$
and none otherwise. This follows either from the scalar equation or by <integration by parts> against $\phi$. It is the compatibility obstruction at <Dirichlet resonance for a point interaction>.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

Use a <Green function> satisfying $L_xG(x,\xi)=\delta(x-\xi)$ with homogeneous <Dirichlet boundary conditions> $G(0,\xi)=G(\pi,\xi)=0$, for $0<\xi<\pi$. Since $G_{xx}=0$ away from $x=1$ and $x=\xi$, it is continuous and piecewise affine, with possible derivative jumps at those two locations.

For $\xi\ne1$, the <transmission conditions for a delta coefficient> and the unit source condition are
$$
\begin{aligned}
[G]_1&=0,&[G_x]_1&=-G(1,\xi),\\
[G]_\xi&=0,&[G_x]_\xi&=1.
\end{aligned}
$$
If $\xi=1$, there is a single junction, and the correct combined condition is
$$
\boxed{[G]_1=0,\qquad [G_x]_1+G(1,1)=1.}
$$
For example, a suitable piecewise form when $0<\xi<1$ is
$$
G(x,\xi)=\begin{cases}A x,&0\leq x\leq\xi,\\B x+C,&\xi\leq x\leq1,\\D(\pi-x),&1\leq x\leq\pi,\end{cases}
$$
where the coefficients depend on $\xi$. The two continuity conditions, $B-A=1$ and $-D-B=-G(1,\xi)$ determine them. When $1<\xi<\pi$, use the same affine form on the intervals cut at $1,\xi$, with $B-A=-G(1,\xi)$ and $-D-B=1$ instead. This gives all requested boundary and jump conditions without needing the coefficients.

An explicit compact kernel also makes the construction transparent. The left and right homogeneous solutions at $\alpha=1$ are
$$
u(x)=\begin{cases}x,&x\leq1,\\1,&x\geq1,\end{cases}
\qquad
v(x)=\begin{cases}1+(\pi-2)x,&x\leq1,\\\pi-x,&x\geq1.\end{cases}
$$
They obey the interaction jump and have <Wronskian> $uv'-u'v=-1$ on either side. Therefore
$$
\boxed{G(x,\xi)=-u\bigl(\min(x,\xi)\bigr)v\bigl(\max(x,\xi)\bigr).}
$$
Differentiating its two branches at the source gives a unit derivative jump whenever $\xi\ne1$. At $\xi=1$, $G(1,1)=-(\pi-1)$ and $[G_x]_1=\pi$, so the combined condition is also satisfied. This is the <Dirichlet Green function for a point interaction> specialized to the present parameters.

The Green kernel itself has zero endpoint values. The prescribed nonzero endpoint values enter through the homogeneous lift:
$$
\boxed{y(x)=c_1v(x)+c_2u(x)+\int_0^\pi G(x,\xi)f(\xi)\,d\xi.}
$$
Indeed, $v(0)=u(\pi)=1$ and $u(0)=v(\pi)=0$. The kernel accounts for the forcing; the lift accounts for the boundary data. Uniqueness follows from part (i), since $1\ne\pi/(\pi-1)$.

